  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1214 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThe dog days of summer are in full swing across the Mid-South.\nUpper riding will continue to build in from the Southern Plains\nand become centered over the Mid-South by Thursday, remaining\nrelatively stationary through the weekend. This will result in\nhighs in the 90s with dewpoints in the low to mid 70s. This will\nproduce heat index readings above 105F across the region with\nvalues of 110F or greater across the Delta through the end of the\nweek. Extreme Heat Warnings and Heat Advisories are in effect for\nthe entire Mid-South through 8 pm Friday and will likely be\ncontinued through the weekend into next week.\n\nShortwaves moving through the northwest flow in place to the\nnortheast of the Mid-South are triggering storms across the Mid\nMississippi Valley and Ohio Valley. An outflow boundary\nassociated with that convection could reach the Mid-South later\ntoday and this evening and trigger a few storms given the amount\nof instability across the region. There is a Marginal Risk (risk\n1 of 5) across portions of West Tennessee near the Tennessee\nRiver and along the KY/TN border. The main threat from these\nstorms would be damaging winds. Cannot rule out a few diurnal\nstorms elsewhere this afternoon as some of the CAMs are showing\ndevelopment.\n\nAs surface winds turn more SW/W this weekend there is a slight\nlowering of the surface dewpoints into the upper 60s to lower 70s\nrange. However, this really will not provide much relief since\nthe slightly lower dewpoints will allow surface temperatures to\nclimb a little higher with some lower 100s likely this weekend\ninto early next week. Any way you slice it, the weather will be\nvery hot so plan for it and take precautions like spending as\nmuch time in the AC as possible. If you have to work outside,\nstay hydrated and take frequent breaks.\n\nThe ridge weakens very slightly by Tuesday and Wednesday as a\nweak cold front tries to  approach from the Ohio Valley. This\nfeature may knock temperatures down slightly over parts of West\nTennessee and might kick off some storms but given the strength\nof the ridge confidence in a break in the heat is low. Global\nmodels show the ridge quickly restrengthening after the middle of\nnext week with the heatwave continuing.",
